


# The Wrong Potion — Day 1: The Revelation

*Transformation Status: Skin softening, body hair thinning, stubble fading, nipple sensitivity dramatically increased. No visible structural changes yet. Clothed, nobody would notice. Naked — Aldric notices everything.*

---

## Early Morning — The Discovery

Something pulled me out of sleep.

Not pain. Not sound. Not Mira shifting beside me or the creak of the Broken Antler settling into its foundation the way old buildings do, groaning and popping like an arthritic man getting out of a chair. Something else. Something internal. A wrongness in my own skin that my sleeping brain had detected and my waking brain was now scrambling to identify.

Tingling. Across my entire body. Concentrated at the surface, everywhere at once, like every nerve ending was firing at a slightly higher frequency than yesterday. The same heightened sensitivity I'd noticed before sleep, but amplified overnight — the blanket against my arms wasn't just sharper now, it was almost abrasive, the coarse wool weave pressing against skin that felt thinner, more receptive, as if someone had peeled away a layer I'd never known was there.

I lay still. Cataloging. The mattress beneath me was more detailed than any mattress had a right to be — I could feel individual lumps, depressions, the architecture of a sleeping surface I'd been oblivious to for a week. The linen of my undershirt against my chest had weight. Texture. Presence. And beneath it — beneath the fabric, against the skin of my chest — a tenderness. Not painful. Just... aware. Aware of being touched by something as mundane as my own clothing.

I sat up.

Mira was still asleep. Curled on her side, her cropped copper hair a bright spot against the gray pillow, her breathing slow and deep. The room was the same. The water stain on the ceiling was still shaped like a horse. The cracks in the plaster were still there. Morning light — thin, pre-dawn, the color of weak tea — filtered through the shutters.

Everything was normal.

Everything except me.

I swung my legs out of bed and stood. My ankle barely hurt — the sprain that had been a swollen nightmare two days ago was now a mild ache, the kind you could walk on without thinking about it. The calf wound was closed, the stitches pulling slightly against skin that was pink and healthy and healing at a speed that defied everything I knew about how bodies worked. Two days of recovery compressed into one night. The poultice Ysolde had applied was crumbling away from the ankle, its work apparently done.

Impressive. Unnatural. And increasingly suspicious.

I went to the mirror.

The polished tin threw back my face — warped, imprecise, the same cheap reflection I'd been staring at for a week. I leaned in close. Turned my face in the gray light.

My skin.

The roughness was gone. Not diminished — gone. The weathered, sun-damaged quality that came from a decade of outdoor living — sleeping in the open, riding in the wind, fighting in the rain — had been stripped away overnight like old paint from a wall. My cheek, when I touched it, was smooth. Not baby-smooth, not dramatically transformed, but noticeably, undeniably smoother than yesterday. Than the day before. Than any day I could remember since I was fifteen.

I ran my hand along my jaw. The stubble was there — I could feel it — but it was wrong. Thinner. Patches of it were sparse where they should have been thick, the three-day growth that should have been heading toward a proper shadow barely qualifying as a light dusting in places. Along the upper jaw, below where the sideburns should have been filling in, there were spots of smooth skin where nothing was growing at all. Not irritated skin, not razor-bumps — just absence. As if the follicles had quietly packed up and left.

I lifted my arm. Held it up to the light from the window. My forearm hair — the dark, coarse hair I'd had since puberty, the hair that had always been there, unremarkable and unremarked upon — was still present. But it was lighter. Finer. I had to look closely, tilting my arm in the light, but several patches were noticeably thinner, the individual hairs transformed from coarse wire to something softer, almost downy. The kind of arm hair a boy might have before puberty finished its work. Or the kind a woman might have, fine and pale and barely visible.

My fingers went to my lips. They were the same shape — full lower, thinner upper — but they felt different under my fingertips. Fuller. Slightly swollen. Softer. More sensitive. I could feel my own pulse in them, a throb of blood close to the surface that I'd never been aware of before.

My heart was beating faster now. Not fast — I wasn't panicking, not yet — but the steady background rhythm had picked up, the body's warning system activating before the conscious mind had finished assembling the evidence.

I lifted my tunic.

My chest. The flat, hard pectorals with their thatch of dark hair. Still my chest. Still the architecture I'd built through years of sword work — thick slabs of muscle, defined, functional. But the hair. The hair was thinner. Visibly thinner. I could see more skin through it than yesterday, the dark strands sparse enough in places that the pale skin beneath was exposed like ground showing through a thinning forest.

And the nipples.

I touched the right one. Lightly. A fingertip, barely any pressure.

The sensation hit me like a fist.

A jolt — electric, immediate, overwhelming — shot from the nipple straight down through my chest and into my stomach and kept going, a hot wire of feeling that reached my groin and sparked there, a flash of something between pleasure and panic. My breath caught. My hand flew away from my chest as if I'd touched a hot stove.

I'd never felt that. Not from a casual touch. Not from any touch. My nipples had always been just... there. Functional anatomy that served no particular purpose and generated no particular sensation. Decoration. Background. Now one light press of a fingertip had sent a signal so intense my whole body had responded, my cock twitching, my stomach clenching, my breathing gone shallow.

I stared at my reflection in the warped tin mirror. The face staring back at me was my face. The jaw was mine. The green eyes were mine. The scar through the left eyebrow — the one constant, the one landmark I could always count on — was mine.

But the skin was smoother. The stubble was thinner. The lips were fuller. The chest hair was sparser. And the nipples were wired to something deep inside me that had never existed before.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What the fuck.
<</dialog>>

I said it to the mirror. To the empty room. To whatever was happening inside me that I couldn't see and couldn't stop and couldn't name.

The mirror didn't answer. Mirrors never do. They just show you what you don't want to see and let you deal with it yourself.

