## Morning — The Confrontation

Mira woke to the sound of pacing.

This was notable because I didn't pace. Pacing was Mira's territory — her anxiety expression, the way she processed worry through motion. I was the one who sat still and stewed, internalizing problems until they either resolved themselves or exploded. The fact that I was wearing a groove in the floorboards of our room told her more than any words could have.

She opened her eyes. Watched me cross the room and back. Three times. Four. The amber eyes tracking me like she was sighting down a drawn bow.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You're either going to tell me what's wrong or you're going to wear a groove in the floor. Pick one.
<</dialog>>

I stopped. Turned to face her. My expression must have been something new, because her eyebrows pulled together in a way I'd never seen — not the professional assessment look, not the amused tolerance, but genuine concern. The expression of a woman seeing something on a man's face that she couldn't classify.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Something's happening to me.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Happening how?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
My skin. My hair. My — look.
<</dialog>>

I crossed to the bed and held out my arm. She sat up, glanced at it with the half-interested attention of someone being shown a mildly interesting rock.

<<dialog "Mira">>
What am I looking at?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The hair. Look at the hair. It's thinner.
<</dialog>>

She took my forearm, turned it in the light, ran her thumb across it once. Shrugged.

<<dialog "Mira">>
It looks like an arm, Aldric. Your arm. The same arm that's been attached to you since birth. What am I supposed to be seeing?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's THINNER. The hair is finer than yesterday. And the skin underneath — feel it. It's smoother.
<</dialog>>

She pressed her fingertips against my forearm. The expression on her face was the one she wore when I pointed out a cloud that looked like a horse, or when I tried to explain why the blacksmith's folding technique was interesting — polite tolerance bordering on active disbelief.

<<dialog "Mira">>
It feels like an arm. A normal, human, slightly hairy arm. You're being paranoid.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not being paranoid. Something is happening. My skin is different. My stubble is patchy. My—
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Your stubble is patchy because you've been bleeding, starving, and drinking that witch's healing compound for two days. Your body is recovering from nearly losing a leg. Things get weird when you're healing. I once knew a man who lost all his toenails after a bad fever. They grew back. He was fine.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
This isn't toenails, Mira.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
No, it's arm hair. Which is even less concerning than toenails. You're exhausted. You're stressed. You nearly died in a goblin mine two nights ago. Your brain is looking for problems because it's not used to having survived something without a new crisis to replace it.
<</dialog>>

I pulled up my tunic. Showed her the chest. The thinning hair. The smoother skin between the pectorals.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Look at this. Tell me that's normal.
<</dialog>>

She looked. Tilted her head. Squinted in the morning light.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I think you need breakfast. And maybe a nap. And definitely to stop examining yourself in mirrors, because that's a road that leads nowhere healthy.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Mira, I'm serious—
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
So am I. You're fine. You look the same as yesterday. Maybe a little cleaner, which frankly is an improvement. The witch's compound is healing your leg at double speed — maybe it's giving your skin a nice polish while it's at it. Consider it a bonus.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Touch my nipple.
<</dialog>>

The longest pause of our entire partnership.

<<dialog "Mira">>
...Excuse me?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Just — touch it. Lightly. I need you to see what happens.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Aldric, if this is some elaborate scheme to get me to play with your chest at seven in the morning, there are easier ways to ask.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
It's not — just DO it. Please.
<</dialog>>

She reached out, her expression caught between amusement and concern, and pressed a finger against my right nipple. Light touch. Barely there.

I flinched. Hard. The jolt shot through me — electric, immediate — and the sound that came out of my mouth was not a sound I'd ever made. Not voluntarily. A sharp, stuttered inhale that was half gasp and half something else entirely.

She pulled her hand back.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Okay. That's... that's new.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
THAT'S what I'm trying to tell you. That never happened before. They've never been sensitive like that. Something is wrong with me.
<</dialog>>

She stared at my chest for a moment. Then at my face. The dismissive humor was still there, but underneath it — for the first time — a flicker of something else. Not alarm. Not yet. Just... attention. The shift from tolerant amusement to actually listening.

<<dialog "Mira">>
It could be the healing compound. Strong alchemical agents mess with nerve sensitivity. I've seen it before — a man in Thornwall took a draught for a broken collarbone and couldn't feel his left hand for a week. Side effects happen.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
And if it's not a side effect?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then we go back to the witch and ask her what she put in you. Simple. But let's not panic about arm hair and ticklish nipples before we've even had breakfast. You're probably fine. You look fine. You SMELL different, but that might just be because you haven't bathed in three days.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I smell different?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Cleaner. Less like a man who sleeps in his armor and more like someone who's recently discovered soap. Which, again, could be the compound doing its thing. Can we eat now? I'm starving and you're spiraling.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I'm not spiraling.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You showed me your chest and asked me to touch your nipples before sunrise. That's spiraling by any reasonable definition. Get dressed. Breakfast. Then — if you're still convinced something is wrong — we'll go see the witch.
<</dialog>>

She stood up. Started pulling on her boots. Matter-of-fact. Dismissive. The body language of a woman who'd heard the concern, categorized it as "probably nothing," and moved on to the practical business of food and planning.

But I saw her glance at my arm one more time as she laced her boots. Quick. Almost imperceptible. The kind of glance you give something when you want to check it again without admitting you're checking.

She hadn't seen what I'd seen. Or she had, and she wasn't ready to admit it.

Either way, we were going to see Ysolde.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Get dressed. We're going to the cistern.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm already getting dressed. I was getting dressed before you asked. Anticipation is a survival skill. And for the record — you're fine. You're going to feel very foolish about the nipple thing when the compound wears off.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
I hope so.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You will. Now stop pacing. You're making me nervous, and I don't do nervous.
<</dialog>>

She said it lightly. Casually. The arrowhead came out — from under the pillow — and clicked against her canines. Click. Click. Click.

But she didn't look at my arm again. She deliberately didn't look at my arm again. And that deliberate not-looking told me more than any examination would have.

