## Mid-Morning — The Apprentice

The Undercity was different in daylight. The sinister quality — the menacing shadows, the sense of hidden dangers lurking in every doorway — was replaced by something more mundane and equally depressing. Cramped. Damp. Tired. The narrow streets that had felt threatening by torchlight were merely sad in the gray morning sun, the buildings leaning inward like drunks supporting each other on the walk home.

My leg was steady as we descended the stone steps into the cistern district. The ankle barely protested. The calf wound pulled but didn't throb. My body was healing at an impossible rate while simultaneously doing something to itself that I couldn't name and didn't want to name and was going to have to name very soon because the evidence was accumulating faster than my ability to deny it.

We found the door. Same weathered wood. Same unremarkable stone wall. Same fermenting-something smell from the building next door.

Mira knocked. Three, two, one. The pattern.

Nothing.

She knocked again. Waited. The silence was thick and specific — the silence of a space that was empty, not just quiet.

<<dialog "Mira">>
She's not here.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
She has to be here. Where else would she—
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
She's not here. I can hear it. An empty room sounds different from an occupied one. The air is still. No breathing, no movement, no presence.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Your ears can tell you the room is empty through a closed door?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Half-elf ears. Enhanced hearing is the only inheritance from my mother's side that's actually useful. The room is empty. Or whoever's in there isn't breathing, which presents a different set of problems.
<</dialog>>

She tried the handle. Locked.

We looked at each other.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Around back?
<</dialog>>

There was no around back — the building was wedged tight between the chandler's shop and the fermenting establishment with no gap. But Mira found a side passage, barely wide enough for one person to squeeze through sideways, that led along the wall to a secondary entrance. A smaller door, set lower, half-hidden by a pile of empty crates that suggested regular deliveries. This door was unlocked.

We entered.

The cellar workshop was exactly as I remembered — stone walls, low ceiling, shelves packed with bottles and jars, the heavy wooden table in the center scarred and stained with use, the smell of herbs and antiseptic. The oil lamps were out but enough light filtered through small high windows to see by. Everything was in its place. Everything was the same.

Except Ysolde wasn't here.

Someone else was.

She sat at a small desk in the corner — a desk I hadn't noticed during my midnight visit, tucked behind a shelf of ceramic jars and half-buried under books, scrolls, and loose papers. She was young — barely twenty, maybe younger — with mousy brown hair pulled into a practical knot that was losing its battle with several escaped strands. Ink smudges on her fingers and her right cheek. Round shoulders hunched over a mortar and pestle half-full of something green. The squinting, focused expression of someone who'd been reading by candlelight for too long and hadn't noticed the sun had come up.

She startled when we came through the door. The mortar nearly went off the desk, caught at the last second by reflexes that suggested this wasn't the first time she'd been surprised while grinding.

<<dialog "Nella">>
We're closed. The practice is — I mean, Mistress Ysolde isn't — we're not taking patients today.
<</dialog>>

She looked at me more carefully. Her eyes — brown, sharp behind the squint — traveled over my face with an attention that was more clinical than social.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Wait. Are you the one from two nights ago? The splinter extraction?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
That's me.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The calf wound, right? Twelve sutures, grade-three foreign body contamination, partial ankle ligament tear. Mistress Ysolde noted you in the treatment log. Eight splinter fragments extracted, antiseptic irrigation, amber numbing compound, green restorative administered orally. How's the healing? Can I see the wound?
<</dialog>>

She recited the details from memory — or from notes so recently reviewed they might as well have been memory. The clinical precision was startling from someone who looked like she should be studying at a university rather than grinding herbs in an underground cellar.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Where is Ysolde?
<</dialog>>

Mira's tone left no room for small talk, case histories, or follow-up appointments. It was the voice she used when she wanted information and wanted it now, stripped of pleasantries and delivered at speed. The voice that preceded arrows.

The girl straightened. Drew herself up to a height that was still several inches shorter than either of us.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I'm Nella. Mistress Ysolde's apprentice. She's — she's not here.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We can see that. Where?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
She left yesterday morning. She does this sometimes. Leaves for weeks, sometimes months. Research trips. Reagent sourcing. She doesn't always tell me where she's going. She left me instructions for ongoing patients and enough prepared compounds to cover standard treatments. Is there a problem with the healing?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
The compound. The green vial. The "restorative compound." What was in it?
<</dialog>>

Nella's fidgeting stopped. Her hands went still on the mortar and pestle. She looked at me — really looked, with the same evaluating gaze Ysolde had used, though younger and less practiced. Her eyes traveled over my face, my arms, my hands resting at my sides. She frowned.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Can you remove your tunic?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Why?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
Because I need to see what the compound is doing. The symptoms you're presenting — the smoothness of your facial skin, the patchiness of your stubble — those are consistent with a specific category of alchemical agent, and I need a fuller picture to confirm. Please.
<</dialog>>

Something in her tone — the shift from flustered apprentice to focused practitioner, the same transformation I'd seen in Ysolde when she'd gone from opening the door to diagnosing my leg — convinced me. This girl might be young and nervous and surrounded by too many books, but she knew her craft. Or at least her mistress's craft.

I pulled the tunic over my head. Stood in the lamplight.

What followed was the most thorough physical examination I'd ever experienced — and I'd been examined by field medics, temple healers, and a veterinarian once in Kestwick when the human doctor was drunk and the horse doctor was sober.

Nella examined my forearm first — running her fingers against the grain of the thinning hair, noting the texture, the density, the way the individual strands had transformed from coarse to fine. She pressed the skin on my cheek. Checked the elasticity by pinching the skin on the back of my hand and timing how quickly it snapped back. Touched a nipple — I flinched, hard — and noted the reaction with a clinical detachment that would have been impressive if I hadn't been standing half-naked in a dead witch's cellar having my nipples professionally assessed.

She lifted my arm and examined the skin of my underarm. Checked behind my ear. Asked me to open my mouth — checked my tongue, the inside of my cheek. She smelled my breath, which was the most awkward medical procedure I'd ever been subjected to, including the veterinarian.

Throughout the examination, her frown deepened steadily. Layer by layer. Like sediment settling on a riverbed, each new observation adding weight to a conclusion she was building in her head and clearly didn't want to share.

<<dialog "Nella">>
When did you first notice changes?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
This morning. But last night, my skin felt different. More sensitive. Everything felt amplified — textures, temperature, touch.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
And the hair thinning? When did that start?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Today. Maybe yesterday. I wasn't — I wasn't paying close attention to my arm hair before this morning. You don't, usually.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
No. You don't. Not until you have reason to.
<</dialog>>

She stepped back. Went to the shelves, scanned them with the rapid eye of someone who knew exactly which book she needed, and pulled down a heavy leather-bound volume — thick, worn, the spine cracked from frequent opening. She carried it to the central table and flipped through the pages with a speed that said she'd been to this section before, or at least knew the neighborhood.

She found the page. Read it. Her face went through several expressions in rapid succession — confusion, recognition, alarm — and then settled into a grim, professional neutrality that was far too composed for a girl her age. That composure was learned. Practiced. The face her mistress wore.

<<dialog "Nella">>
The compound Mistress Ysolde gave you was not a standard restorative.
<</dialog>>

The room went very quiet. The kind of quiet that has weight. The kind that presses against your ears and makes the silence itself feel like a sound.

<<dialog "Mira">>
What was it?
<</dialog>>

Nella set the book on the table, open to a page dense with handwritten notes — Ysolde's hand, precise and angular — and a detailed alchemical diagram. The diagram showed a human figure. Male on the left side. Female on the right. Arrows and annotations tracked a process of change between the two, with timeline markers and chemical notations that meant nothing to me and clearly meant everything to Nella.

<<dialog "Nella">>
It's a feminization compound.
<</dialog>>

The word hit the room like a stone dropped into still water.

<<dialog "Nella">>
A full-spectrum alchemical transformation agent. It rewrites the body's fundamental template — skeletal structure, musculature, fat distribution, hormonal balance, primary and secondary sexual characteristics. Everything. The changes progress over approximately thirty days, advancing incrementally through distinct phases. What you're experiencing now — the skin softening, the hair reduction, the increased nipple sensitivity — that's the earliest phase. The foundational changes. The compound is preparing your body for what comes next.
<</dialog>>

Silence. A very long silence. The kind of silence that entire lives pivot around — the before-silence and the after-silence, with one sentence sitting between them like a wall.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're telling me that your mistress fed me a potion that turns men into women.
<</dialog>>

My voice was flat. Careful. The voice of a man standing at the edge of a very high cliff and choosing his next step with extraordinary deliberation.

<<dialog "Nella">>
A very sophisticated one, yes. From what I can see in her research notes, it's a proprietary formula. Years of development. Possibly decades. The changes are gradual — they progress through distinct stages over the thirty-day cycle, each phase building on the previous one. What you're experiencing now is Day 1. The beginning. The most subtle phase.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What comes after the subtle phase?
<</dialog>>

Nella took a breath. The breath of someone delivering news they've rehearsed but still don't want to say.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Over the coming days and weeks, the transformation will accelerate and become more comprehensive. Your bone structure will change — jaw narrowing, cheekbones shifting, overall facial feminization. Your muscle mass will redistribute from the upper body to the lower body. Your waist will narrow. Your hips will widen.
<</dialog>>

She paused. Cleared her throat.

<<dialog "Nella">>
You'll develop breasts. Your voice will change — the larynx will restructure, raising your vocal pitch. Your body hair will continue to thin and eventually disappear from most areas. Your skin will soften further. Your height will decrease slightly as the skeletal frame compacts. Your hands and feet will shrink. And your... your primary sexual characteristics will be affected. Eventually. Significantly. By the end of the full cycle — approximately Day 30 — the transformation will be complete. You will be, physically, entirely female.
<</dialog>>

Another silence. Longer this time. Heavier.

I was standing shirtless in a cellar. A girl half my age was telling me I was going to grow breasts. The absurdity of it collided with the horror of it and neither won. They just stood there, side by side, staring at each other across the wreckage of everything I'd assumed about my future.

Mira spoke first. She'd gone very still during Nella's explanation — the motionless calm of someone whose mind was racing behind a locked expression, processing information at a speed that her body refused to reflect.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Can it be reversed?
<</dialog>>

Nella nodded. Carefully — not wanting to give false hope, not wanting to take real hope away. Walking the line between the two with the precision of someone trained to deliver medical information that could change lives.

<<dialog "Nella">>
Yes. An anti-compound exists — or should exist. Any alchemist developing a transformation agent of this complexity would create a counter-agent. It's standard practice in advanced alchemy. The reversal can be done at any stage — before, during, or after the transformation completes. Even if you reach Day 30 and the cycle finishes fully, the anti-compound can still reverse it. The transformation is not permanent.
<</dialog>>

Something loosened in my chest. A knot I hadn't known was there, wound so tight it had become part of the background tension of my body, suddenly released a fraction of an inch. Not permanent. Reversible. The door was open. I just had to find the key.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
So it's not permanent.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
The transformation itself is not inherently permanent, no.
<</dialog>>

She held up a hand. The "but" was coming. I could see it in her eyes — the careful positioning, the measured delivery of information that was about to take the hope she'd just given and put conditions on it.

<<dialog "Nella">>
But. Only Mistress Ysolde has the anti-compound formula. Only she can create it. This is proprietary, master-level alchemy — the kind of work that takes decades of research and access to extremely rare reagents. No other alchemist in Valdmere — probably no other alchemist in the region — could reverse-engineer the counter-agent without years of study and access to her original research. So while reversal is theoretically possible at any point, it is practically impossible without her.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
And she's gone.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
She left yesterday morning. Before — before you would have noticed the effects. The timing is...
<</dialog>>

She trailed off. The implication settled over the room like frost forming on glass — slow, visible, and cold.

<<dialog "Mira">>
She knew. She knew what the potion would do and she left before he could come back.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
I don't think the timing is coincidental. No. I think she... I think she planned this. Though I don't understand why. Mistress Ysolde has always been — she's not cruel. She's helped hundreds of people. I've watched her save lives that temple healers would have given up on. Whatever her reasons for this, they're not malicious. Or at least — I don't believe they are. But I also can't explain them.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Her motivations are fascinating. Where did she go?
<</dialog>>

Mira's voice had the edge now. The blade-voice. The one she used before violence — tight, controlled, each word placed with the precision of an arrow.

<<dialog "Nella">>
I don't know exactly. But—
<</dialog>>

She went to the desk and rummaged through a stack of papers. Pulled out a folded note and a small leather journal, the pages filled with Ysolde's angular handwriting.

<<dialog "Nella">>
She left me instructions for the practice. Patient schedules, compound preparations, ongoing treatments. And in the margins, she mentioned needing to visit the Thornfield Priory — it's a ruined monastery about five days' travel northeast, in the foothills of the Ashenmire range. She goes there periodically for rare reagents that grow in the monastery's old gardens. She also mentioned the Blackthorn Market — a seasonal trader's fair near the town of Corsham, about seven days northeast. It's possible she's heading to one or both.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Five to seven days northeast. On foot?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
On foot, yes. With a cart, maybe four.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Does she have a cart?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Nella">>
She has a mule. She always takes the mule when she travels.
<</dialog>>

Mira looked at me. I looked at Mira. The conversation happened without words — the silent language we'd developed over three months of working together, refined through dozens of jobs and hundreds of shared meals and one unforgettable horse cart:

*Her:* We're going after her.

*Me:* Obviously.

*Her:* You're going to be changing while we travel.

*Me:* I know.

*Her:* Every day. More changes. More visible.

*Me:* I know.

*Her:* Are you okay?

*Me:* No. Let's go anyway.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
