<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
If I hadn't done it, you'd be dead. Or a man with one leg.
<</dialog>>

She pressed her lips against my chest. The center. Where the hair was thinnest now.

<<dialog "Mira">>
At least this way, you're alive. Whatever happens to your body — whatever it becomes — you're alive. And I'd rather have you alive and female than dead and male.
<</dialog>>

A pause.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Don't tell anyone I said that. I have a reputation.
<</dialog>>

I almost laughed. Almost. It came out as something between a laugh and a sigh — the sound of a man standing at the edge of something vast and dark and finding, against all odds, that the person beside him wasn't going to let him fall.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We'll find her. We'll find the witch and we'll get the antidote and we'll fix this.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
And if we don't find her in time?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Then I'll teach you how to bind your chest and we'll deal with it.
<</dialog>>

She kissed my shoulder. The bite mark. Tender now, where it had been fierce minutes ago. The same spot, the same mouth, but a different intent — not claiming, just caring.

<<dialog "Mira">>
Now sleep. We have a journey to plan.
<</dialog>>

---

## Night — Planning and Sleep

We couldn't quite sleep. The nervous energy of tomorrow's departure kept us both alert, the adrenaline of the day's revelations still circulating through our bloodstreams like the residue of a storm that had passed but hadn't finished with the sky.

We sat up in bed. Mira drew a rough route map on the back of a bread wrapper — the only paper we had that wasn't the Consortium contract or Grent's napkin map. The Ashenmoor Road east from Valdmere. The surveyor's ruins site at two days. The Corsham crossroads where the road branched northeast. The Thornfield Priory in the foothills, five days from the city. The Blackthorn Market, seven days, further northeast.

<<dialog "Mira">>
We need to figure out how to explain your changing appearance to the surveyor. He's going to notice. Over three days of travel, he's going to notice.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Alchemical accident. Exposed to an unstable compound during a job. Side effects are cosmetic and temporary.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Good. Simple. Doesn't invite questions. What about clothing?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
What about it?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
We should bring the extra tunic. The loose one. And—
<</dialog>>

She eyed my chest.

<<dialog "Mira">>
—things that fit loosely. In general.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Mira.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm being practical!
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
You're enjoying this.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm not enjoying it. I'm—
<</dialog>>

She stopped. Her hand went still on the bread wrapper. The arrowhead stopped clicking. Something shifted behind her eyes — the humor draining away, the sarcasm peeling back, and underneath it: the thing she'd been covering all day with jokes and efficiency and the relentless forward motion of a woman who dealt with fear by refusing to hold still long enough for it to catch her.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'm coping. This is how I cope. I make jokes and I plan and I don't think about the thing I can't control. If I think about the thing I can't control, I'll—
<</dialog>>

She stopped again. Her amber eyes were bright in the candlelight. Brighter than usual. Not tears — Mira didn't cry, or at least she'd never cried where I could see — but something adjacent. The moisture that accumulates when the emotion behind the eyes is too large for the eyes to contain.

<<dialog "Mira">>
I'll be scared. And that's not useful right now.
<</dialog>>

I reached over and took her hand. My palm against hers — her calluses against my calluses, my calluses that were softer than yesterday, smoother, the skin already changing under her touch. Her archer's hand gripping my swordsman's hand, and the swordsman's hand beginning its quiet transformation into something neither of us had a name for yet.

<<dialog "Aldric">>
We'll be okay.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You don't know that.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
No. But I'm saying it anyway. Because that's what you do when someone you — when someone you're traveling with is scared. You say the thing, even if you're not sure it's true.
<</dialog>>

She squeezed my hand. Held it. In the candlelight, her face was the face I knew — sharp, striking, the amber eyes and the cropped copper hair and the jaw that set like a drawn blade when she was determined. The face of the most competent person I'd ever met.

<<dialog "Mira">>
You were going to say something else. Instead of "traveling with."
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Was I?
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
You were. You changed it mid-sentence.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Aldric">>
Must have been the ale.
<</dialog>>

<<dialog "Mira">>
Must have been.
<</dialog>>

She blew out the candle. We curled together in the dark — her body against my back, her arm across my waist, her breath on the nape of my neck. The position we'd settled into. The arrangement that had become a foundation. The one thing that the compound couldn't change, because it lived between us rather than inside either one of us.

My chest was tingling. A low-grade sensation — pins and needles but softer, concentrated around the nipples and the flat tissue behind them. Something was happening in there. Cells dividing. Tissue beginning its slow accumulation. The very first stirrings of a change that would transform my chest over the coming weeks from the flat planes of a swordsman to something curved and soft and undeniably female.

I could feel it starting. Not the change itself — not yet, too subtle for that — but the *potential* of it. The gathering of forces beneath the skin, like clouds building before a storm. The slow, patient work of becoming someone else.

Tomorrow I would wake up and the mirror would show me more. Each morning from now — more. Less of the man I was. More of the woman I was becoming. Each day a step further from the body I'd known and closer to a body I'd never imagined.

I didn't sleep for a long time.

When I did, I dreamed.

A tall woman with iron-gray hair and dark, depthless eyes, standing at a crossroads in a forest. The road split before her — two paths, each disappearing into trees. She held a vial in each hand. One green, the liquid shifting inside it like something alive. One clear, the contents still and pure as water from a mountain spring.

She held them out to me. Her smile was small and patient. The gardener's smile. The seed-watcher's smile.

<<dialog "Ysolde">>
Choose.
<</dialog>>

I reached for the clear one. My hand extended, fingers stretching toward the antidote, the reversal, the return to everything I'd been.

The dream ended before I could touch it.

I woke in the dark, Mira's arm warm across my waist, my chest tingling with the slow, patient work of transformation.

The clear vial was somewhere northeast, on a mule, in the hands of a woman who'd smiled while she changed my life.

I was going to find her. I was going to find the vial. And I was going to choose.

But first, I had to survive the journey. And the journey was going to change me in ways I couldn't imagine yet.

Tomorrow: the road. The surveyor. The first day of travel with a body that was slowly becoming a stranger.

Day 1 was over. Twenty-nine remained.

---

*[End of Day 1 — Day 2 begins: The journey northeast. The changes accelerate. The road does not care who you are or who you're becoming.]*